#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176932} :activity/id #uuid "fb65b447-b251-5b8b-84fb-25df81ffc211" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049895 :topic/id #uuid "1c611a71-aaa1-5a41-a6cd-aa83bbba97af" :topic/math-academy-id 3645} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177088 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the following linear transformation: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, it can only be one of the following:  \n\n- the whole of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$\n\n- a line through the origin, or\n\n- a single point (vector), namely the origin $(0,0).$\n\nLet's denote the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ by $T.$ It turns out that the dimension of the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ depends on $\\text{rank}(T)$ (or equivalently on $\\text{nullity}(T)$ by the rank-nullity theorem). Indeed, we have the following:\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=2,$ then the two columns of $T$ are linearly independent. This means $T$ is invertible and therefore, $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) =  \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto  $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7d/7de193042587cb9362641cbb541477a5e4951e96d9e82f074fb0cc3a86a80d3a.svg)\n\n![](images/88/88baa47cae14d4a43d59b366c13b3c29376678edef2fe34ff505a942ef6af986.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=1,$ then there is a nonzero vector that generates the image of $\\mathbf{T}.$ In this case, the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a one-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^2$. So, it is a line passing through the origin.\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=2x\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/49/4952615b0c4ddf85cc6d60483f8c0587093c67734a320cde913644fe8fdafb55.svg)\nNotice that the points $(2,4)$ and $(1,2),$ defined by the columns of $T,$ lie on the line $y=2x.$\n\n![](images/03/0322f183c16fe82515277d10cf27a93ae753f8eadf7eeb1db4d3c5e404480b09.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=0$ then,  according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that  $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})=\\mathbf{0}$ for all $\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Bbb R^2$. So, in this case, we have that\n![](images/4a/4a0ce67cffc02e28edffb9efeedca2e21ffc7c0d8b72ccfa0566cb29f6679865.svg)\nwhich maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\{(0,0)\\},$ i.e., the set containing only the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n![](images/12/1252907a1e9767201dd326945f328f91fc493ea283b244dbfee42a7c4248d9e6.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0ec0551e-64f7-52a7-a0e7-827e6a8944f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8344 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "79dd6a5b-b38e-5027-95bc-e8b46a7f3a61" :step/math-academy-id 70373 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176087 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175719 :question/id #uuid "954c2d7b-19d8-584b-aeb5-0927c79e2a65" :question/math-academy-id "e-13332"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1fb22de-94c2-51a9-803b-f2bd77bcb012" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049539}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492824}] :knowledge-point/title "Identitying the Type of the Image of a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "b9da32ac-2fba-5b76-a770-d303f55e3aff" :step/math-academy-id 70374 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175720 :question/id #uuid "91058f47-ebdb-5a02-ba3d-de01