:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176160}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554744} :activity/id #uuid "aec8459e-928a-54f2-bdee-00c8aea7fe8b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049895 :topic/id #uuid "1c611a71-aaa1-5a41-a6cd-aa83bbba97af" :topic/math-academy-id 3645} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d07d408-4bd4-52a2-b369-1432edfe2c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469020} {:answer/id #uuid "eaeb8c45-a223-54ca-b996-e4cc9a54fe8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469021}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469020} :answer-field/id #uuid "246b7ed5-a7fd-53be-80d4-b0d726f0fda5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f53549c-840d-53f3-bf45-f0d90bcc6ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469022} {:answer/id #uuid "215f631a-fd6a-5cc0-906f-13e0a5fbd25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469023} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a6bf326-d27f-5130-ad2f-4b066b4f2754" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470123} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb3b5e2-0da0-57b0-a807-697b0aeddec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469024} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fd395f-d3c4-53ed-b990-698626ffd03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469024} :answer-field/id #uuid "435f823d-9228-557b-a231-fc7e35bab06c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "34581764-4087-5b06-9eff-4679fe703405" :question/math-academy-id "q-38300" :question/problem "If the linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-3x$ then, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨1,3⟩$ belongs to the range of $T$\n\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n\n- **III.** $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. \nNotice that the point $(1,3)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=-3x$ since $3\\ne -3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,3⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \nsince $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line passing through the origin, we have that the standard matrix $T$ is singular. Hence, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. \nFor example,  the point $(1,-3)$ satisfies the equation  $y=-3x$ since $-3=-3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,-3⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$ Thus, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {0}.$"} :step/id #uuid "4e1cc83d-73ad-5afc-aeed-1408d33e5a20" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554736}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ef481a-705d-5f82-9a19-92ceb01cfee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366761} {:answer/id #uuid "7a54ef0a-64c9-5842-8675-a775bf4e73ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366762} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f8f571-4006-5417-916b-8b1ccb869c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366763} {:answer/id #uuid "52efde24-9a00-5780-9f50-bdae742b41f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366764} {:answer/id #uuid "fccc2756-9459-5499-8770-f77a30708a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366763} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ca72594-5be6-5643-b9f7-7c9ff9fc7016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eff617ee-6ce3-51e8-bcd8-61c31ebef8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97068" :question/problem "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(1,4)$ and $(2,8),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8-4}{2-1}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=4x.$"} :step/id #uuid "ab78c80f-43b1-5e4a-b1ce-b10f78c76970" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12eb12db-c865-5a15-995f-57d1e99856b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452323} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2992f7-296f-52e8-8e2f-b1875fa14a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452323} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ebab7b-febe-5850-8be3-97869ab43f9b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dff232c6-f90e-549a-bf5d-1544c0d7eb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452325} {:answer/id #uuid "cc22eb64-3d36-5f56-935a-3d6d0d508e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452326} :answer-field/id #uuid "9583cd23-de18-5d81-9215-33038a6df612" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d092dba5-3f4c-5126-93c1-b72bf8d37079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452327} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4acc6c-2043-51df-a00b-c80988412bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452327} :answer-field/id #uuid "5957978d-2e1f-5607-8d63-c7b81149356e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2335439b-17b4-5a3d-b1ce-91063205bf0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97079" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨2,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1,\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $(2,1)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=2x$ since $1\\ne 2\\cdot 2.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n⟨2,1⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ce998921-646a-57c6-8ac9-ac0f89d7c0db"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f40948d-6a2c-590f-8a11-24b48ddadb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366754} {:answer/id #uuid "0f77d896-8399-547f-8c3f-2490b781e097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366755} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ca92a7-2ef3-53a1-8ddd-54bea50ff463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366756} {:answer/id #uuid "963c7644-d7cd-502e-9404-7dfcb73af315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366757} {:answer/id #uuid "c8765811-b618-5407-abfc-5f01d72ccdd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366758} :answer-field/id #uuid "292d5352-84c3-580e-88ff-42f528f9a4e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1afcec6c-a31f-5b1b-826d-d0232bfbe82b" :question/math-academy-id "q-97034" :question/problem "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(1,-1)$ and $(-1,1),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1-(-1)}{-1-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-x.$"} :step/id #uuid "d2f2b9cb-b3c2-59aa-97dd-0e695b49c9ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec8c126b-beb0-5259-95a8-ee03317b9dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477289} {:answer/id #uuid "05b4a708-dd67-557a-9e5d-3cec0dfaf56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477289} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9fdd20-93bb-54d2-8f45-dd434f374b95" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477614} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca155e7-6b82-5264-aff3-a5b9bbf22322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477291} {:answer/id #uuid "d241b648-c419-53a1-bd11-5a4510ba9eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477291} :answer-field/id #uuid "8474779a-6aa5-57d6-8c8a-638b08fec6e2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477615} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85d7e293-6acb-57a3-84e4-89d7e0daf5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477293} {:answer/id #uuid "c9be56df-604d-5bf1-b89a-a5381de8206d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477294} :answer-field/id #uuid "6637f2a2-acef-52c2-99ff-897ed538dbfe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4acbb26f-7e4b-53ce-a37d-bd74763516c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38297" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=1$,  where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$\n- **II.** $\\text{Im}(T)$ contains no vectors of ${R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The image of $T$ contains the vector zero vector $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ea3324c3-6bf7-53ce-b75e-4da72766cf63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554674}}] :activity/title "The Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553853}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564545} :activity/id #uuid "9ea48e0e-20fc-57ae-8fc6-7c2c77548e20" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049895 :topic/id #uuid "1c611a71-aaa1-5a41-a6cd-aa83bbba97af" :topic/math-academy-id 3645} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564545 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175bf4de-4457-5893-9e70-48b016ba1c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564001} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc44505-66d5-5275-ab67-b9b56ad13597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564070} {:answer/id #uuid "51381d98-4b2c-5d35-b328-a422e2a36439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564098} {:answer/id #uuid "53c0aaa8-38d2-5451-8a27-0bab44551579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564103} {:answer/id #uuid "9049bcb9-bcf9-552e-88ca-aa47961cecd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564070} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1ac556-d4ef-5630-9e88-eb68dd60c483" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "950ea572-58c1-514e-8c7a-a1bc5bc62c33" :question/math-academy-id "q-97038" :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(2,2),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2-0}{2-0}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=x.$"} :step/id #uuid "1855919c-4174-5759-93cf-11a0280966ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "874ac1f7-0153-507d-91ce-fd04d90ca247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173897} {:answer/id #uuid "02f0558b-35a2-5e4d-a4f1-1d0d49844653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173898} {:answer/id #uuid "04310fd5-0111-5f80-b616-19512cf451da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173899} {:answer/id #uuid "061f9b11-0217-5760-abc1-a8399b5515c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173900} {:answer/id #uuid "45945036-7b0c-58a7-8eb0-c915cb38566e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173900} :answer-field/id #uuid "55c9a14f-7b63-589d-a835-3fb38b9d252c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86bc6bae-46fe-5b21-aa49-70bad1578afa" :question/math-academy-id "q-111791" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(T)=0$\n\nIII. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "33095b71-1333-5602-a459-f3798f52eba0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd0d15c-be74-50b4-a48c-b8617532b07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564007} {:answer/id #uuid "620a4056-288f-5313-ad88-b1b0f5b5687b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564122} {:answer/id #uuid "63ce0689-59e9-51d3-8840-c15ea78dfa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564125} {:answer/id #uuid "a12b6a7f-7604-5711-86fc-b635e4b043ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564213} {:answer/id #uuid "aee37cb9-5834-51b9-a3fe-6104df070ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564238} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfa28e82-e7a7-5631-a9aa-0b573881c151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d1c2efa-cbed-55e3-967d-daf6bf46e742" :question/math-academy-id "q-97069" :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(2,-6)$ and $(-1,3),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-2}=-3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-3x.$"} :step/id #uuid "521a6f9a-99a3-57bc-b710-6c05998470da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dda9e3d7-e6ee-5825-845d-f01fdb4e4b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398083} {:answer/id #uuid "cdba0602-2fd0-52ae-8f15-118e4a6f97e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398084} {:answer/id #uuid "f2332fcb-d82d-5111-a699-5798822a8e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398085} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf49b0b-ca8d-5df2-944e-152ab812be35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398086} {:answer/id #uuid "75145a95-95a2-5300-a3b1-4c2ffbe7d0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398084} :answer-field/id #uuid "39de4ad4-8ca2-5e4e-a502-b05807674875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "96dc0ffa-c035-5c4a-b42f-7a4928dfb8df" :question/math-academy-id "q-111789" :question/problem "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(2,-1),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-1-0}{2-0}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x.$"} :step/id #uuid "af32582e-7c99-52e2-a82b-8a159c337dcb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564552}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48d21b03-b4aa-5edf-97d9-e8d82ba76aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454250} {:answer/id #uuid "a4df005b-76b4-5349-9381-8a02c4118a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454250} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0c8fb9-1191-5fbe-89aa-082fed4cfe5c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46db31c3-b479-5b1f-9f99-dea7a4e5f9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454252} {:answer/id #uuid "6adfd406-7a7b-520c-b675-6259bd0b2894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454252} :answer-field/id #uuid "61692f77-337b-53e8-9104-052d1bee29b9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bf8b14e-2ea8-5937-a61f-4fbf1ad7451e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454254} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5d6c2c-7493-5064-bdb2-fd8b5146ef90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454255} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfa9f97-b5ce-506a-a8e7-d728d9426d04" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5aa9ee11-e88d-5123-8f62-331a18227a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97075" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(T)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "cea9ce61-7dee-5a91-9c27-e1a1933f13fb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564581}}] :activity/title "The Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563964}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572244} :activity/id #uuid "bb29ac7c-738b-5774-a724-5debacd67403" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049895 :topic/id #uuid "1c611a71-aaa1-5a41-a6cd-aa83bbba97af" :topic/math-academy-id 3645} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572211 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175bf4de-4457-5893-9e70-48b016ba1c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564001} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc44505-66d5-5275-ab67-b9b56ad13597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564070} {:answer/id #uuid "51381d98-4b2c-5d35-b328-a422e2a36439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564098} {:answer/id #uuid "53c0aaa8-38d2-5451-8a27-0bab44551579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564103} {:answer/id #uuid "9049bcb9-bcf9-552e-88ca-aa47961cecd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564070} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1ac556-d4ef-5630-9e88-eb68dd60c483" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "950ea572-58c1-514e-8c7a-a1bc5bc62c33" :question/math-academy-id "q-97038" :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(2,2),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2-0}{2-0}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=x.$"} :step/id #uuid "1acebc51-ffe4-5f71-9738-b7991922c9ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572230}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b324769-9430-5f5c-80c0-9c13d075c858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492307} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca6c8b6-2f80-5c70-9069-f0586e112d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492366} {:answer/id #uuid "88430c53-2ff5-5c57-bc6b-a66b7136b41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492487} {:answer/id #uuid "a0602523-5644-52d8-bb00-13c7914d03e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492537} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cca849-8dd3-5d33-8394-2e0d4cedf5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492559} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0672c6-a8a1-54ee-b67e-0f22d1bf3419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ea7759f-f796-5f54-9f1a-8b557e8042a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-111788" :question/problem "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(1,3),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{3-0}{1-0}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=3x.$"} :step/id #uuid "589eeb11-b0b7-55ed-ad3a-22e0a871e976" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572244 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a942b5d1-7b1a-5fc4-bd44-8277a11d5922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174989} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c68240-98d9-514f-9240-be81ecbac1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174990} {:answer/id #uuid "e747930b-6ed3-5180-ad7b-d7d4f0fb64db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174991} {:answer/id #uuid "298996ce-0479-5e8f-8843-c67a96f5c06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174992} {:answer/id #uuid "43722a77-85c3-5aa4-a12d-664a740790d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174993}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4366e1f6-501e-5781-bcac-0878c93b7f0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9fe1b78-433c-504f-a583-950509a9b7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395893} {:answer/id #uuid "0108eb21-5320-5ff8-96d7-8f8de5fcef1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395894} {:answer/id #uuid "bf907f2a-341b-5baa-a98d-8d7e0ddb910a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395895} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd9ad35-c837-57f3-ade6-3a3db7427a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395896} {:answer/id #uuid "e20ea794-ad35-5073-91ed-1a4d6170f723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395895} :answer-field/id #uuid "de87dec5-6939-5a03-ab02-65e0d9863a39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "326587d3-f0fa-5430-a120-481fbe187c4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-38229" :question/problem "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(1,3)$ and $(2,6),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{6-3}{2-1}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=3x.$"} :step/id #uuid "757d3965-2bf7-5ecb-8cc7-3977b2a1468d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429cbe16-f5e3-5043-b6c8-816c129c4f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571659} {:answer/id #uuid "44066267-1187-5ad1-ae24-12c19959375d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571661} {:answer/id #uuid "98ea171d-32ec-53f1-bbbd-33159ec58a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571804} {:answer/id #uuid "af071dd2-7bf2-5139-91db-22d623177322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571841} {:answer/id #uuid "c376bd76-aedb-536d-88ca-18c1489c93b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571659} :answer-field/id #uuid "b089493b-c4d4-51a9-971f-914da25cc8a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b0a0a680-c34b-55b6-a46f-cafc346112a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-38231" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=6x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $⟨2,\\,12⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n\nII. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the point $(2,12)$ satisfies the equation of the line $y=6x$ since $12=6\\cdot 2.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n⟨2,\\,12⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9b33180f-7eae-599e-bc1e-994f9654e026" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572211}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48355326-ad4b-578d-906e-404260be62dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453288} {:answer/id #uuid "137c30dc-eac8-5144-aa6e-ecac88c88768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453289} :answer-field/id #uuid "029df452-27e1-55c2-95cc-c51c4094c660" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454394} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c79a7166-446d-5b5a-a95f-e732499d72f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453290} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2b1e1d-e940-5f7a-a610-b38db6adeb6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453290} :answer-field/id #uuid "e492fc36-ff9b-5ce9-b63f-47f732639a39" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454395} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f22ea7c-9234-582e-9fd8-6c4d53049067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453292} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae5ae4c-d9b5-5c1e-8df4-fc71fc9b7598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453293} :answer-field/id #uuid "21f48fb4-a9ca-5657-b58f-f9b05523a3e0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d9c3015-ea78-542d-9de3-a1c266f8369d" :question/math-academy-id "q-111793" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨1,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $T$ is surjective and so  $⟨1,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f6e1e1b7-efcb-5597-9c08-b9a62e35c074"}] :activity/title "The Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571533}]}
